    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-31163",
      "knowledge_point_id": "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=11-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
