    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-31153",
      "knowledge_point_id": "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)$ at the point where $x=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "The slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is given by ${f}^{′}(2).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,u=3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 2+5) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡11.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
