    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-51026",
      "knowledge_point_id": "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170",
      "knowledge_point": "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-1)$ at the point $(1+\\pi ,-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=1+\\pi $ is given by ${f}^{′}(1+\\pi ).$\n\nWe will calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1+\\pi ,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1+\\pi ) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\pi -1) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(1+\\pi ,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =0(x-(1+\\pi )) \\\\ y+2 & =0 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
